{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 7, :comments ["<b>And YHWH Elohim formed the man, dust from the earth.</b> After the Torah mentioned the creation of man in the section of the sixth day in general terms, it returned and explained the matter of the creation in detail in this section. And it clarified here that the creation of which it said, ״ויברא אלהים את האדם בצלמו״ — 'And Elohim created the man in His image' — was the creation of dust from the earth. And it added to explain that after He created and formed him as matter and form, He breathed into him a soul from the spirit of His holiness. And the reason it did not mention above, on the sixth day, ״ויפח באפיו נשמת חיים״ — 'and He breathed into his nostrils the breath of life' — is that He did not wish to mention on the sixth day any attributes of the rational soul at all, but only bodily attributes, which belong solely to the affairs of this world. For the matter of creation and the matter of the blessing of fertility and reproduction, and the matter of dominion and rule, are not the business of the soul but the business of the body and the business of the desiring soul, which ceases when the body ceases. Therefore He mentioned only the attributes of his body, because from the side of his body he is generated and perishes like the beasts and the wild animals that were created with him on that same day; and there was no place to mention on that day the attributes of his soul, which is eternal and everlasting, among the souls of the dead — until the six days of Creation were completed, which are the six days of death. And only then did He mention it after the seventh day, which is the day of life." "<b>And by way of</b> the Kabbalah: ״וייצר ה' אלהים״ — 'And YHWH Elohim formed' — and ״ויטע ה' אלהים״ — 'And YHWH Elohim planted' — this is the intent of the forceful verse (I Chronicles 4:23): ״המה היוצרים ויושבי נטעים וגדרה עם המלך במלאכתו ישבו שם״ — 'These are the potters, and those who dwell among plantations and hedges; with the king in his work they dwelt there.' And so the Sages, may their memory be blessed, interpreted in Bereshit Rabbah: Rabbi Levi said, He took counsel with the souls of the righteous, as it is said, ״המה היוצרים״ — 'these are the potters'; ויושבי נטעים — 'those who dwell among plantations' — המה היוצרים, alluding to ״וייצר ה' אלהים״; יושבי נטעים, after ״ויטע ה' אלהים״ — end of quote." "<b>And you should</b> acquire understanding and comprehend that above, in the utterance ״נעשה אדם״ — 'Let us make man' — it says ״ויברא אלהים את האדם בצלמו״ — 'And Elohim created the man in His image' — and it did not mention dust at all, nor did it say, 'Let us make man from the earth.' For all the matters of the work of Creation are revealed and concealed: explicated in the lower realms and alluded to in the higher realms. And because of this, you must understand the concealed from the revealed, and you will understand the intent of the verse ״זה ספר תולדות אדם ביום ברוא אלהים״ — 'This is the book of the generations of man on the day Elohim created' — why it mentioned this twice. And here, in this verse, it mentioned dust and earth. And likewise you should be roused to ask about the two verses that follow this one, which are ״ויטע״ — 'and He planted' — and ״ויצמח״ — 'and it sprouted': ויטע alludes to the higher realms, and ויצמח explicated in the lower realms. Therefore, in the verse ויטע it did not mention earth, nor tree, nor fruit; whereas in the verse ויצמח it mentioned earth, tree, and fruit. And behold, this is as clear before you as the sun, that the matters of all the work of Creation are doubled, and the generations of the heavens and the earth are bound together, these with those, as I have explained above." "<b>Dust from the earth.</b> The first hour: his dust was gathered. The second: it was kneaded. The third: it was shaped. The fourth: a soul was cast into him. The fifth: he stood on his feet. The sixth: he named names. The seventh: Eve was paired with him. The eighth: two went up and four came down. The ninth: he was commanded. The tenth: he sinned. The eleventh: he was judged. The twelfth: he was expelled and went his way, as it is said (Psalms 49:13): ״ואדם ביקר בל ילין נמשל כבהמות נדמו״ — 'And man, though in honor, does not abide; he is like the beasts that perish.' Thus they interpreted in Sanhedrin, at the end of the chapter 'Aḥad Diné Mamonot'; and likewise in Pirkei Rabbi Eliezer. And the matter is that the order of the hours in the creation of man — its completion and its perfection — corresponds to the order of the six days of Creation. The first hour, in which his dust was gathered, corresponds to ״יהי אור״ — 'Let there be light' — when the emanation was intermingled and nothing was separated from anything else. The second, in which a boundary was set for the gathered dust — where the head would be, or the legs, and the rest of the limbs — corresponds to ״יהי רקיע״ — 'Let there be a firmament' — which placed a sign within the waters to perform operations upon them, like a man building a ruin who marks: this is a room, and this is an upper story. The third, in which a delineation of limbs was drawn in him and the limbs were revealed, corresponds to the third day, when the dry land was revealed and took form, as it is said: ״ותראה היבשה״ — 'and the dry land appeared.' The fourth: a soul was cast into him, corresponding to the luminaries; and so the soul is the light of the body. The fifth: he stood, corresponding to the angels, who were created on the fifth day and are called 'standing ones,' as it is said (Zechariah 3): ״בין העומדים האלה״ — 'among these standing ones.' The sixth: he named names, corresponding to the sixth day, which it was. The seventh: Eve was paired with him, corresponding to the seventh day, which is Sabbath — she was paired with him below, as Israel is her counterpart above." "<b>And they said</b> in the Midrash Rabbah: ״עפר מן האדמה״ — 'dust from the earth.' Rav Huna said: עפר is masculine and אדמה is feminine. This potter brings עפר, masculine, and אדמה, feminine, so that his vessel will be sound. And the meaning of the Midrash is that all created beings, all possessors of matter and all living beings, are male and female — even in the plant and in the inanimate. Another interpretation: ״עפר מן האדמה״ — from the site of the Temple, from the place of his atonement, was he created. It is written here ״מן האדמה״, and it is written there (Deuteronomy 32): ״וכפר אדמתו עמו״ — 'and He will atone His land with His people'; and likewise (Exodus 20): ״מזבח אדמה תעשה לי״ — 'An altar of earth you shall make for Me.'" "<b>And you should know</b> that from here he was created and to here he returned when he rotted and was banished from the Garden of Eden — that is what is written: ״לעבוד את האדמה אשר לוקח משם״ (to work the earth from which he was taken). And here he settled and established his descendants, and this is what Cain said: ״הן גרשת אותי היום מעל פני האדמה״ (Behold, You have driven me out this day from the face of the earth). And he further added: ״ומפניך אסתר״ — and from Your presence I shall hide — meaning, from the place of Your indwelling Presence; and this is like the language of what is written (Psalms 24): ״מבקשי פניך״ (those who seek Your face); (Psalms 27): ״את פניך ה׳ אבקש״ (Your face, HaShem, do I seek); (Psalms 105:4): ״בקשו פניו תמיד״ (seek His face always). And this is the meaning of what it mentions: ״ויצא קין מלפני ה׳״ — Cain went out from before HaShem — in the same language said of Jonah the prophet (Jonah 1:3): ״ויקם יונה לברוח תרשישה מלפני ה׳״ (And Jonah arose to flee to Tarshish from before HaShem), for his intention was to depart from the place where the Shekhinah dwells, so that the spirit of prophecy would not rest upon him and he would not be commanded to go on that mission." "<b>And He breathed into his nostrils the breath of life.</b> Know that the nose is the instrument of the breath (נשמה), for through it the breath enters and through it the breath goes out; therefore our Rabbis, of blessed memory, required one who has been crushed by a collapse to be checked up to his nose, as it says: ״כל אשר נשמת רוח חיים באפיו״ — all in whose nostrils is the breath of the spirit of life. And among the wonders of the formation of man is that you find the sign of the letter שי״ן inscribed in this instrument — from the name שדי (Shaddai) — engraved upon the form of the body of every circumcised one; and this is what is written (Job 32:8): ״ונשמת שדי תבינם״ (and the breath of Shaddai gives them understanding). It is known that this is the instrument of smell, for the breath (נשמה) derives pleasure from fragrance; therefore our Rabbis, of blessed memory, forbade the fragrance of idolatry, for it is not fitting that the breath (נשמה) derive pleasure from the remembrance of idolatry, for the fragrance of any thing is its remembrance — in the manner of what is written regarding the offerings (Leviticus 6:8): ״ריח ניחוח אזכרתה לה׳״ — a fragrant odor, a remembrance offering to HaShem. And understand this." "<b>And you need</b> to know that the philosophers have disagreed concerning the nature of the soul and have composed on this subject books without end, elaborating and expanding their language extensively. Some of them say that the soul is one and within it are three faculties: the appetitive faculty, which belongs to the possessors of the animal soul; the vegetative faculty, which belongs to the possessors of the vegetative soul; and the rational faculty, which is the soul of wisdom — and these three faculties are one soul. And some of them say that in man there are three distinct souls, different one from another: the rational soul by itself, the vegetative by itself, and the animal by itself. The animal soul is the appetitive soul — desiring food and drink and intercourse and sleep and anger; through it man shares with beasts and animals and all possessors of the soul of motion; its dwelling is in the liver, and it is called נפש (nefesh) and רוח (ru'aḥ), in the manner of what is written (Deuteronomy 12:20): ״כי תאוה נפשך לאכול בשר״ (when your nefesh desires to eat meat); and it is written (Ecclesiastes 7:9): ״אל תבהל ברוחך לכעוס״ (do not be hasty in your ru'aḥ to anger); and it is written (Proverbs 29:11): ״כל רוחו יוציא כסיל״ (the fool vents all his ru'aḥ). The vegetative soul is the faculty that causes growth in man; through it he shares with the trees and the plants; and the function of this soul is to grow the body of the plant and the tree, raising it and widening it, and likewise in man; and its dwelling is not in one particular place in the body but in all of it, and it prevails until a fixed time. The rational soul is found in man alone; through it he shares with the upper holy ones who stand forever in wisdom and understanding; its dwelling is in the brain, and it is called נשמה (neshamah), as it says: ״ויפח באפיו נשמת חיים״ (and He breathed into his nostrils the neshamah of life); and it is written (Isaiah 42:5): ״נותן נשמה לעם עליה״ (who gives neshamah to the people upon it); and it is written (ibid. 57): ״ונשמות אני עשיתי״ (and the neshamot I have made); and it is written (Job 32:8): ״ונשמת שדי תבינם״ (and the neshamah of Shaddai gives them understanding). And according to the opinion of these sages who hold that in man there are three distinct souls, we find a proof for our Rabbis, of blessed memory, from what they said: Rava created a man and sent him before Rabbi Zeira; he [Rabbi Zeira] was speaking with him but he [the created man] did not speak back; he said to him: ״דמן חבריא את — תוב לעפרך״ — you are from the associates [of Sefer Yetzirah]; return to your dust. This statement demonstrates that all three are in man as distinct souls, different one from another, for Rava, through his vast wisdom in which he delved deeply into the wisdom of Sefer Yetzirah, created that man and implanted in him the soul of motion, but he did not have the power to place speech in him, for speech comes from the side of the rational soul." "<b>However,</b> this verse proves the opinion of those who say that the soul is one and within it are three faculties, and they are not distinct souls, for the body of the first man lay like an inanimate stone with no movement at all — that is what is said: ״וייצר ה׳ אלהים את האדם עפר מן האדמה״ (and YHWH Elohim formed the man, dust from the earth), and as soon as He breathed into his nostrils this breath (neshamah): ״ויהי האדם לנפש חיה״ — and the man became a living soul — he was set in motion through it, like the beasts and the fish, of whom it is said: ״שרץ נפש חיה״ (swarming living souls). And this is the meaning of ״חיה״ (living): for with this breath of life (נשמת חיים), the man returned to become a living soul, whereas beforehand he lay like an inanimate stone with no movement in him. And whether according to this opinion or that opinion — whether the soul is one with three faculties, or whether they are three distinct souls — all agree on the survival of the rational soul after separation from the body." "<b>And already</b> the philosophers have brought on this subject clear and great proofs and many decisive demonstrations; I shall mention two of them. The first demonstration: that this rational soul knows all things, far and near, above and below, without the sight of the eyes, without the hearing of the ears, and without the other senses — for the five senses belong only to the animal soul, serving it as tools in the hand of the craftsman. For the eye sees the sphere of the sun as the size of its apparent height, but this rational soul knows that it is one hundred and seventy times larger than the earth, as the great master our Rabbi Moses ben Maimon wrote at the beginning of the Book of Knowledge, chapter three. And the eye sees in water and in a mirror many forms, and this rational soul knows that they are accidental and not real. And the ear hears sounds and voices in different places, and this rational soul knows that they are incidental. And if the five senses belonged to the rational soul and it perceived things through them or through some of them, the remaining extremities would be nullified and false, of no use — but there is nothing false in the rational soul, and there is no extremity it does not know, for it knows all things according to their true nature and their truth. And the rational soul's presence in the body is like a sailor on a ship, for when separated from the body it endures and is not lost, just as the sailor endures when he separates from the ship. But the animal soul is intermingled with the body; therefore upon separation from the body it perishes with the body's perishing, for the animal soul and the vegetative soul cannot perform any act, small or great, without the body in which it dwells, for all their actions are in the body, and upon the body's perishing they perish — both they and their actions — because each of them has matter, and anything that has matter cannot perform its actions except through its matter. But this rational soul performs its actions without the body, for it knows the intelligibles of the sphere and what is above them, and the affairs of the earth and what is beneath it, because it is intellect and has no matter. And whenever it wishes to know composite things, it knows them through the five senses that belong to the animal soul; and when it wishes to know simple things, near and far, it knows them through its own intellect and wisdom. And upon separation from the body, it knows only simple things, near and far, since it no longer has the five senses through which it grasped composite things, for they have already been lost from it. And all this is a proof and demonstration that the soul endures after the death of the body.\n\nThe second demonstration is what we see with our eyes: that man in his beginning, when he is young, knows nothing; and when he grows and increasingly grows, the intelligibles multiply in the soul and knowledge increases. And what the soul acquires of wisdom and knowledge must derive from one of three parts — there is no fourth: either from his body alone, or from his soul alone, or from his body and soul together. If it were from his body, it would follow that the more a man's body is healthier and stronger and the more matter increases in him, the wiser he would be and the more prepared to receive intelligibles; and one whose body is emaciated and whose matter is slight would be more foolish and more remote from receiving intelligibles. But we see the opposite, for someone languishing, or one afflicted with consumption — his body diminishes every day while his intellect stands at its perfection until his soul departs from him. This proof nullifies the notion that the acquisition of wisdom and knowledge derives from his body, and by this very proof the notion that it derives from his body and soul together is likewise nullified. If so, all of man's acquisition of wisdom and knowledge is from the soul alone, and the body has no share in this except that it is an instrument for her, like the instrument for the craftsman. And since the existence of wisdom and knowledge is impossible among the dead, yet must necessarily exist among the living — behold, the soul is therefore alive by nature, for it is in its nature to receive wisdoms, while the body is dead by nature and it is not in its nature to receive anything of this. It has thus been demonstrated by proof that man is composed of two essences: one of them alive by nature — and that is the soul — and the second dead by nature — and that is the body. And because they joined together, there befell each one an accident on account of its companion: there befell the body the vitality of sensation on account of the soul, and there befell the soul death, which is the punishment, on account of the body that misleads it. Behold, the soul is therefore alive by nature and dies accidentally, and the body is dead by nature and lives accidentally. And when each of the two is separated from its companion, there is united with the body the complete death which is its nature, and separated from it is the accidental vitality it acquired from the soul; and there is united with the soul the complete vitality which is its nature, and separated from it is the accidental death that befell it because of its immersion in the body.\n\nAnd although we have spoken this little bit concerning matters of the soul and brought these proofs from the words of the philosophers and some of their decisive and apparent demonstrations that find rest in the heart, the fundamental principle is only our holy Torah, which is the source of life; from it all who delve deeply into wisdom drew, and all their wisdom is nothing but the fruits of the Torah. And the matter of the soul's eternal survival after separation from the body is explained from this verse that says ״נשמת חיים״ — breath of life — meaning, a breath hewn from the source of life, for the breath (neshamah) is hewn from the source of wisdom; that is what is written (Job 32:7): ״אמרתי ימים ידברו ורוב שנים יודיעו חכמה אכן רוח היא באנוש״ (I said, days should speak, and the multitude of years should teach wisdom; but it is a spirit in man) — Scripture attached wisdom to spirit (רוח) to explain that the spirit derives from the source of wisdom. And from this the verse here mentions ״נשמת חיים״ — breath of life — on account of what is written (Ecclesiastes 7:12): ״והחכמה תחיה בעליה״ (and wisdom gives life to those who possess it). And this is what the Holy One, blessed be He, promised the prophet: that his soul would be in the company of the holy angels through the fulfillment of the commandments — that is what is written (Zechariah 3:7): ״אם בדרכי תלך ואם את משמרתי תשמור וגם אתה תדין את ביתי וגם תשמור את חצרי ונתתי לך מהלכים בין העומדים האלה״ (If you walk in My ways and if you keep My charge, then you shall also judge My house and also keep My courts, and I will give you access among these who stand by).\n\nAnd if you understand the verse that Solomon said in his wisdom, then you will understand the excellence of the soul and the ascent of its rank and its foundation and its secret — that is what he said (Ecclesiastes 3:19): ״ומותר האדם מן הבהמה אין״ (and the preeminence of man over the beast is nothing) — he explained that man's advantage over the beast is from the aspect of the thing he called אין (nothing, i.e., non-material), and that is from the aspect of the rational soul within him, drawn from wisdom, which is the יו״ד united with the will, which is the אל״ף; and understand this very well. And from this the expression ״ויפח באפיו״ (and He breathed into his nostrils) was used, for from it one understands the foundation of the soul and the greatness of its excellence, in that it is hewn from the spirit of holiness. Therefore it is compared to the Holy One, blessed be He, in five things, and it resembles Him in all its attributes, and it is greater than the angels, for the soul was created on the first day and the angels on the second; and it is known that the superiority and subtlety of the upper created beings is according to their precedence in creation, and thus they said explicitly: ״גדולים צדיקים יותר ממלאכי השרת״ — the righteous are greater than the ministering angels. The conclusion of the matter: all that the mouth can speak of the excellence of the attributes of the Holy One, blessed be He, according to what He is the Creator of all — is included in the attributes of the soul according to what she is a creator. And the sage said: ״דעו נפשותיכם תדעו אלהיכם״ — know your souls and you will know your Elohim. And know all this." "<b>And the man became a living soul.</b> You will not find in all the work of Creation that the word 'and it was' (ויהי) recurs with the created thing, except in the creation of light and of man, concerning which Scripture employs the language of ויהי — as it is written, ״ויהי אור״ and ״ויהי האדם לנפש חיה״ — to teach that the soul of man is eternally enduring like the supernal light, and is destined to delight in its radiance. And you already know the saying of our Rabbis of blessed memory, that the souls were created on the first day; and from this the liturgical poet said: 'He established the souls together with the first light, the beginning of the word of HaShem.' And the plain sense of the verse is that the essential being of man is solely for the sake of the living soul."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 2, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:10:36.039621705Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}